<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><office:document xmlns:office="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:office:1.0" xmlns:style="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:style:1.0" xmlns:text="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:text:1.0" xmlns:draw="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:drawing:1.0" xmlns:fo="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:xsl-fo-compatible:1.0" xmlns:svg="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:svg-compatible:1.0" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:presentation="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:presentation:1.0" office:version="1.2" office:mimetype="application/vnd.oasis.opendocument.presentation"><office:styles/><office:automatic-styles><style:style style:name="text" style:family="graphic"><style:graphic-properties draw:stroke="none" draw:fill="none" fo:padding="0cm"/></style:style><style:style style:name="navy" style:family="graphic"><style:graphic-properties draw:stroke="none" draw:fill="solid" draw:fill-color="#172d40"/></style:style><style:style style:name="pale" style:family="graphic"><style:graphic-properties draw:stroke="none" draw:fill="solid" draw:fill-color="#eaf5f3"/></style:style><style:style style:name="line" style:family="graphic"><style:graphic-properties draw:stroke="none" draw:fill="solid" draw:fill-color="#c7d8db"/></style:style><style:page-layout style:name="layout"><style:page-layout-properties fo:page-width="61cm" fo:page-height="85cm" style:print-orientation="portrait"/></style:page-layout><style:style style:name="S0" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="17pt" fo:color="#ffffff" fo:font-weight="bold"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S1" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="54pt" fo:color="#ffffff" fo:font-weight="bold"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S2" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="22pt" fo:color="#ffffff" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S3" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="17pt" fo:color="#ffffff" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S4" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="16pt" fo:color="#007d80" fo:font-weight="bold"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S5" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="30pt" fo:color="#172d40" fo:font-weight="bold"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S6" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="21pt" fo:color="#172d40" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S7" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="28pt" fo:color="#007d80" fo:font-weight="bold"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S8" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="21pt" fo:color="#172d40" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S9" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="28pt" fo:color="#007d80" fo:font-weight="bold"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S10" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="21pt" fo:color="#172d40" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S11" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="28pt" fo:color="#007d80" fo:font-weight="bold"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S12" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="21pt" fo:color="#172d40" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S13" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="28pt" fo:color="#007d80" fo:font-weight="bold"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S14" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="23pt" fo:color="#172d40" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S15" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="28pt" fo:color="#007d80" fo:font-weight="bold"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S16" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="23pt" fo:color="#172d40" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S17" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="21pt" fo:color="#172d40" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style><style:style style:name="S18" style:family="paragraph"><style:paragraph-properties fo:margin="0cm"/><style:text-properties fo:font-family="Liberation Sans" fo:font-size="13pt" fo:color="#4a5e69" fo:font-weight="normal"/></style:style></office:automatic-styles><office:master-styles><style:master-page style:name="Default" style:page-layout-name="layout"/></office:master-styles><office:body><office:presentation><draw:page draw:name="Poster" draw:master-page-name="Default"><draw:rect draw:style-name="navy" svg:x="0cm" svg:y="0cm" svg:width="61cm" svg:height="12.55cm"/><draw:rect draw:style-name="pale" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="13.4cm" svg:width="57cm" svg:height="6.6cm"/><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="1.2cm" svg:width="56cm" svg:height="1cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S0">NeurReps 2026                                         Symmetry and Geometry in Neural Representations</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="3cm" svg:width="57cm" svg:height="4.7cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S1">Determinants of hyperparameter robustness</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S1">in connectome reservoir computing</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="8.6cm" svg:width="57cm" svg:height="2cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S2">Miles Walter Churchland¹, Raul de Palma Aristides², Jordi Garcia-Ojalvo¹,</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S2">Anna Ritz³, Greg Anderson⁴, Miguel C. Soriano²</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="10.8cm" svg:width="57cm" svg:height="1cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S3">¹Universitat Pompeu Fabra    ²IFISC, CSIC–UIB    ³Biology, Reed College    ⁴Computer Science, Reed College</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2.8cm" svg:y="13.85cm" svg:width="55cm" svg:height="1cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S4">MAIN FINDING</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2.8cm" svg:y="14.7cm" svg:width="55cm" svg:height="2.6cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S5">Higher performance accompanies greater sensitivity</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S5">to hyperparameter choice.</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2.8cm" svg:y="17.5cm" svg:width="55cm" svg:height="2.4cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S6">We compare a C. elegans connectome reservoir with controlled network variants across 96 hyperparameter settings. The connectome occupies a relatively low-variance regime. Changes in excitatory/inhibitory (E/I) edge balance also alter the scaling required by spectral-radius normalization.</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="20.8cm" svg:width="18cm" svg:height="1.4cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S7">01   Connectome as a reservoir</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame svg:x="3.2cm" svg:y="22.3cm" svg:width="15cm" svg:height="8.06cm"><draw:image><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="30.7cm" svg:width="17.5cm" svg:height="15.4cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S8">C. elegans: 297 neurons, 3,604 chemical edges. EleganSign [1].</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">Orange: positive    Blue: negative</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">Largest connected component shown; arrows omitted.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">Fixed recurrent weights; only the readout is trained. Perturb topology, signs, and weights, then normalize spectral radius.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">96 settings × 200 trials</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">Vary target radius, leak rate, input scale, and bias.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">IPC: information processing capacity; nonlinear recall [2].</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">KR: kernel rank; history separation [3].</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">MC: memory capacity; recall of the past 30 inputs.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">CV = sd / mean across the 96 settings.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S8">Lower CV = greater robustness.</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="22cm" svg:y="20.8cm" svg:width="37cm" svg:height="1.4cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S9">02   Performance comes with sensitivity</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame svg:x="22cm" svg:y="22.5cm" svg:width="37cm" svg:height="19.51cm"><draw:image><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="22cm" svg:y="42.4cm" svg:width="37cm" svg:height="3.7cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S10">Higher and farther right: more performance, less robustness. Contours enclose the highest-density 50% of trials; markers identify architectures. Color indicates E/I edge balance: 0%, CE ≈24.25%, or 50%.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S10">CE: C. elegans; ER: Erdős–Rényi; WS: Watts–Strogatz. Sign-pres.: signs preserved; sign shuf.: signs shuffled; pm1: ±1 weights.</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:rect draw:style-name="line" svg:x="20.45cm" svg:y="21cm" svg:width="0.06cm" svg:height="24.5cm"/><draw:rect draw:style-name="line" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="46.5cm" svg:width="57cm" svg:height="0.06cm"/><draw:rect draw:style-name="line" svg:x="32cm" svg:y="47.5cm" svg:width="0.06cm" svg:height="28.5cm"/><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="47.5cm" svg:width="29cm" svg:height="1.5cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S11">03   E/I balance traces the tradeoff</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame svg:x="5cm" svg:y="49.2cm" svg:width="23cm" svg:height="24.23cm"><draw:image><office:binary-data></office:binary-data></draw:image></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="74cm" svg:width="29cm" svg:height="2.5cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S12">Changing signs moves performance and CV together. Top: IPC, KR, and MC across E/I edge balance; black markers denote empirical balance. Bottom: raw radius and CV averaged across metrics. Points average 200 trials.</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="33cm" svg:y="47.5cm" svg:width="26cm" svg:height="1.5cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S13">04   What the curves show</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="33cm" svg:y="49.3cm" svg:width="26cm" svg:height="8.5cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S14">Connectivity and weight magnitudes stay fixed while signs are randomly reassigned at each E/I edge balance.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S14">Near the all-positive and all-negative endpoints: lower performance and lower CV, indicating greater hyperparameter robustness.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S14">Toward 50% E/I edge balance: higher performance and higher CV, indicating greater sensitivity to hyperparameter choice.</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="33cm" svg:y="58cm" svg:width="26cm" svg:height="1.5cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S15">Why normalization matters</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="33cm" svg:y="59.7cm" svg:width="26cm" svg:height="15cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S16">Every perturbed matrix W′ is rescaled:</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S16">Wres = (ρtarget / ρraw) W′,    ρraw = max |λᵢ(W′)|.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S16">The raw radius decreases toward the balanced regime. Reaching the same target radius therefore requires a larger scaling factor.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S16">These curves combine changes in sign balance and placement with normalization-induced rescaling. They do not isolate a causal effect of E/I balance alone.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S16">Report raw spectral radius alongside architecture comparisons.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S16">The full paper finds the same qualitative tradeoff in an edge-matched random topology and after removing unknown or complex-sign edges.</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:rect draw:style-name="pale" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="76.7cm" svg:width="57cm" svg:height="3.4cm"/><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2.7cm" svg:y="77cm" svg:width="55cm" svg:height="3cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S17">Scope and limitations. Robustness means low relative variation across the tested hyperparameter grid. IPC omits products across different delays. Of 3,604 chemical edges, 1,864 have unknown or complex signs and are assigned signs to match the labeled-edge ratio; selected controls remove them. Electrical synapses and self-loops are excluded; input reaches all units.</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame><draw:frame draw:style-name="text" svg:x="2cm" svg:y="81cm" svg:width="57cm" svg:height="3cm"><draw:text-box><text:p text:style-name="S18">Selected references    [1] Fenyves et al. (2020), PLOS Comput. Biol. 16, e1007974.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S18">[2] Dambre et al. (2012), Sci. Rep. 2, 514.</text:p><text:p text:style-name="S18">[3] Roy &amp; Vetterli (2007), EUSIPCO, 606–610.</text:p></draw:text-box></draw:frame></draw:page></office:presentation></office:body></office:document>